     "type": "concept",
     "title": "Value-Based Fees vs. Hourly Billing",
     "content": "Hourly billing is the original sin of professional services. Weiss is blunt: **'The day you bill by the hour is the day you cap your income.'**\n\n**Why hourly punishes excellence:**\n• Get faster — you earn LESS for the same outcome\n• Get smarter — you earn LESS for using one breakthrough you spent a decade learning\n• The client watches the clock — every conversation becomes adversarial\n• Your day has a hard ceiling: ~2,000 billable hours/year, max\n\n**Value-based fees flip the equation:**\nThe fee is set BEFORE the work begins, based on the **value of the outcome** to the client. If the engagement saves $2M, a $200K fee is a 10x return. If you deliver it in 40 hours or 400, the fee is the same.\n\n**This rewards:**\n• Speed (more clients per year)\n• Mastery (you charge for results, not labor)\n• Honesty (no padding hours)\n• Strategic thinking (less time on tasks, more time on insight)\n\nThe shift is psychological before it is contractual. You must believe your insight is worth more than your minutes.",
