     "title": "Building a Model From Scratch: The Client Gravity Framework",
     "content": "To see the five components in action, consider a hypothetical consultant, illustrative rather than real, who specializes in helping professional service firms convert one-time projects into recurring relationships. Working only from experience, she initially describes her work generically as \"client retention consulting\" — a phrase that invites direct price comparison with dozens of similarly described competitors. She decides to build named IP around her actual insight: that clients stay not because of service quality alone but because of accumulated switching cost and relationship depth, a force she starts calling client gravity. First, she names the model: the Client Gravity Framework. Second, she builds a visual: a three-stage funnel labeled Attract, Anchor, and Advance, each stage represented by a widening circle to suggest increasing pull, which she can sketch on a napkin in under a minute. Third, she coins terminology within the model — an \"anchor point\" is any single deliverable so embedded in a client's operations that replacing the consultant would mean redoing real work, and an \"advance trigger\" is a specific signal that a client is ready for expanded scope. Fourth, over eighteen months she collects five documented cases where firms using the framework increased retained-client revenue by a measurable percentage, building the evidence base that turns the model from a nice idea into a demonstrated result. Fifth, she writes a facilitator's guide and trains two junior associates to deliver the diagnostic workshop using her materials, proving the model is teachable beyond her own personal delivery. Within two years, the Client Gravity Framework is licensed to a small network of independent consultants who pay her an annual fee to use her materials and refer clients back to her for advanced engagements — income that no longer depends on her own billable hours.",
